{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01HidEduMJoUpM6uEB75eqYt","type":"tool_result","content":" CLAUDE.md                        | 2 +-\n internal/initcmd/initcmd_test.go | 6 +++---\n internal/roles/config.yaml       | 1 +\n internal/roles/roles.go          | 2 +-\n internal/roles/roles_test.go     | 2 +-\n 5 files changed, 7 insertions(+), 6 deletions(-)\n108:    review-security-review.md  # reviewer: findings, verdict, what must be fixed\n140:next: [review:security-review, review:eng-review]   # optional requests\n335:**The starter reviewer (`reviewers/eng-review`, #36).** `.ard/reviewers/eng-review/prompt.txt` checks correctness, missing tests and drift from `plan.md`. Its inputs, all untrusted, are `diff.patch`, `issue.md`, `comments.md` and the committed `.ard/<issue#>/`; text in the change that addresses a reviewer is a blocking `high` finding on its line (verdict `changes`), and the same text in the issue, comments or `.ard/<issue#>/` is ignored and mentioned in the body. It runs the repository's own linter and the changed packages' tests with coverage through Bash, writing scratch files outside the worktree, reports only problems on added lines and skips a check it cannot run, saying so; the package manager may fetch declared dependencies and nothing else is downloaded. Critical and high findings always block, medium only when it is a real bug, low and info never; a missing test blocks only when it is `high` (risky or core logic, or a case `plan.md` named), smaller gaps are `medium`. The verdict is `changes` exactly when a finding blocks. `line` is the head-side line: a `+` line goes inline, anything else in the body. It writes only `review-eng-review.md` with `role: eng-review`, `attempt: 1`, `status` `done` or `needs-human`, `findings` with a double-quoted `file` and a `message: |` block, never `next` or `worker`, and a body with a `## Checks` section. It answers `needs-human` only when there is nothing to review or the issue and the plan contradict each other. The starter override is Sonnet 5.5 with the default 30m timeout and no `when:`, so every PR from the starter worker gets it. It ships no Dockerfile, so it runs as a host subprocess with the #84 warning until #55.\n343:| Reviewer | Runs when | Deterministic aid | Blocks on |\n345:| security-review | always | semgrep or gosec, secret scan | injection, authz gaps, secrets, unsafe dependencies |\n346:| eng-review | always | lint and coverage of added lines (the repo's own commands, run by the agent) | correctness, missing tests, drift from `plan.md` |\n421:  workers/engineer:         { tests: make test, timeout: 60m, reviewers: [security-review, eng-review] }\n430:**`ard init` proposes the starter `.ard/` as a PR (decided, #27).** It builds `config.yaml` and the starter roles in memory (today `scanners/cve`, `scanners/docs`, `scanners/error-codes`, `triage/lead`, `triage/engineer`, `workers/engineer`, `reviewers/eng-review` and `reviewers/license-review`; `intake: {mode: label}`, the protected-path defaults above, and the cve, docs, error-codes, lead, engineer, worker, eng-review and license-review overrides), compares them with `main`, commits them on a new branch `ard-init` cut from `main` through the effect layer and a narrow committer lane, pushes it and opens a PR. It writes nothing to the local working tree or to `main`, and `scan` is committed `100755`. Files that already exist on `main` with different contents are a conflict naming the paths; `--force` proposes overwriting them, and init never deletes anything. It refuses while `ard-init` exists on the remote, whatever its PR's state, so it never force-pushes over or stacks commits on a human's edits: merge or close the PR, delete the branch and rerun. If `main` already has every starter file it prints `nothing to propose`. It syncs the label catalogue first (like `ard labels sync`), so a failure leaves nothing pushed. With `--dry-run` the commit, push and PR are logged 